    introduction: "Sampling records values at discrete times; ideal sampling alone need not round the amplitude. Quantisation replaces each amplitude by one of finitely many levels and loses information even at a very high sampling rate. For ordinary low-pass signals use fs > 2fmax with analogue anti-alias filtering and practical transition-band margin; equality can fail for arbitrary phase. All quantisers here are uniform unipolar mid-rise: L = 2^n equal intervals cover [0,VFS], codes are unsigned interval indices, reconstruction is the interval midpoint, internal boundary values enter the upper interval, and exact VFS or out-of-range values clamp to the nearest end code. Error means reconstruction minus original input. The half-step error bound applies to in-range inputs including the endpoints, not overload. No uniform-random quantisation-noise or ideal ADC hardware specification is assumed.",
